e('Because every frame is the same size, any free frame can hold any page. There is no searching for a hole that fits, and no external fragmentation.','For a fixed base-page size, any suitable free frame can back a page; an ordinary virtual region need not occupy one physical hole. Physical contiguity still matters for huge pages and some device/kernel allocations.','No globaleliminationphysicalfragmentation.',hu)
e('On x86-64 each entry is 8 bytes:','For ordinary x86-64 4 KiB leaf mappings, entries are 8 bytes. The table summarizes selected fields; implemented physical-address width and enabled CPU features constrain their interpretation:','Leafvsupper/modefeatureconstraints.',sdm)
e('| 8 | G | Global (kernel) |','| 8 | G | Global translation when enabled |','Globalnotkernelpermissionbit.',sdm)
e('| 12–51 | PFN | Frame address |','| 12–51 | PFN field | Address bits, limited by MAXPHYADDR |','NotallCPUs52bitPA.',sdm)
e('The CPU sets **A** whenever the page is used and **D** whenever it is written.','The CPU maintains **A** and **D** for translation use and writes. After clearing them, the OS must use the required invalidation protocol to observe later accesses reliably; cached translations do not promise a fresh in-memory bit update on every access.','TLBcachedAD semantics.',sdm)
e('The OS is free to use the other 63 bits for its own purposes, for example to record where in swap the page was stored.','The OS can encode software state such as swap information in a non-present entry, while respecting architectural and security constraints. Non-present entries must not be treated as unconstrained storage on every implementation.','L1TF/reservedarchitecturalconstraints.',sdm)
e('A write to a page with R/W clear, user access to a page with U/S clear, or an instruction fetch from an NX page also raises a page fault.','For ordinary user-mode accesses with NX support enabled and coherent translations, writing through R/W=0, reading a supervisor-only mapping, or executing an NX mapping faults. Supervisor write protection depends on control state; cached translations must be invalidated after changing permissions.','WP/NXE/TLBscope.',sdm)
e('**512 GiB per process**. Impossible.','**512 GiB** for the full 48-bit space. This is an impractical flat-table overhead for ordinary processes.','Notliterallyimpossibletoday.')
e('| PML4 | PGD | 512 GiB |','| PML4 | PGD (P4D folded) | 512 GiB |','Explicit4levelsoftwarefolding.')
e('The cost grows only with the memory actually mapped:','Ignoring kernel mappings, KPTI and other process regions, this toy count assumes the stack also fits within one 2 MiB region. Table cost depends on mapped address distribution as well as size:','Sparseaddressdistributioncountassumptions.')
e('first shipped in Ice Lake-generation CPUs.','available on supporting CPU models.','Precise firstshipclaimnotneeded/unsourced.',mm)
e('It adds a fifth table (PML5; Linux calls the extra level the P4D) above PML4.','In five-level mode, PML5 is Linux PGD and PML4 is Linux P4D. In four-level mode P4D is folded and PML4 acts as PGD.','OriginalmappednewhardwarePML5toP4Dwrong.',pt)
e('The cost of 5-level paging is one more level on every page walk, which is why it is left off on machines that do not need it.','Five-level paging can add a lookup to an uncached walk. A capable kernel can enable it on supported hardware even for small-memory workloads; unsupported hardware uses the folded four-level configuration.','Notautomaticallyleftoffifnotneeded.',mm)
e('- **Inverted page tables** (IBM POWER historically) keep one entry per *physical frame* and search by hashing the virtual page number. The table size scales with RAM rather than with the address space.','- **Inverted page tables** keep entries indexed by physical frame, with a lookup mechanism such as a hash index over address-space identity and virtual page number. They are distinct from a hashed forward mapping table; not every architecture using hashed translation has one entry per physical frame.','Hashedvsinvertedconflation.')
e('A 1-byte mapping still uses 4 KiB, and on average half a page per region is wasted. Bigger pages waste more.','An independently backed 1-byte region can occupy a whole base page once allocated. Average half-page waste assumes roughly uniform allocation remainders; sharing, lazy allocation and suballocation change the result. Bigger pages can increase waste.','Lazyzero/shared andstatisticalassumptions.')
e('A process mapping 100 GiB with 4 KiB pages needs about 200 MiB of page tables','A dense, populated 100 GiB mapping with 4 KiB pages needs about 200 MiB of leaf page tables','Mappinguntouchednotalltablespopulated.')
e('Hardware that does DMA needs special handling (an IOMMU or physically contiguous buffers).','DMA mappings must meet the device\'s requirements; options include scatter/gather lists, IOMMU translation or physically contiguous buffers.','MissingcommonSGalternative.')
e('- Paging maps fixed-size virtual pages to physical frames, eliminating external fragmentation.','- Paging removes the requirement that an ordinary virtual region fit one physical hole; huge and contiguous allocations can still suffer fragmentation.','Summaryconsistent.',hu)
