f(5,'prompt','A single load stays within a present 4 KiB page on x86-64 with four-level paging. It misses all translation and page-walk caches. Count one read per paging level plus the data read, ignoring accessed-bit updates, nested virtualization and data-cache effects. How many reads does this model require?','Hugepages/crossboundary/ADwritesexcluded.',sdm)
f(5,'workedExample','Read PML4, PDPT, PD and PT entries, then the requested data: five reads in this simplified model. It is not a prediction of 5× wall-clock latency: cache hits, overlapping work and other hardware behavior matter. A five-level full walk adds one entry read.','Memoryaccesscountnotexactlatency.',sdm)
f(6,'prompt','For ordinary user-mode x86-64 accesses, with NX enabled and no stale cached translations, which conditions cause a page fault? Assume canonical addresses and all other permissions permit the access. Select all that apply.','Makefaultanswerkeysunambiguous.',sdm)
f(6,'workedExample','In this model, non-present mappings, writes forbidden by R/W, user accesses forbidden by U/S and execution forbidden by NX fault. Clear A/D bits are bookkeeping state rather than a reason to reject an otherwise permitted access. Hardware-maintained A/D state and permission changes still require correct OS invalidation protocols; this is not a full specification of every paging mode.','A/Dnever/modelscope.',sdm)
