f(7,'workedExample',json.loads(Path(q).read_text())[6]['workedExample'].replace('With 2 MiB huge pages, the PTs vanish: just one PD of 4 KiB. With a 1 GiB page, a single PDPT entry.','With 2 MiB pages, the leaf PTs vanish and one 4 KiB PD covers the region, plus the required upper tables. With a 1 GiB page, one PDPT entry covers it, still within allocated upper tables. Existing tables may be reused.'),'Hugepagesretainuppertables.',hu)
f(10,'text','An uncached multi-level walk to a base-page leaf requires more entry reads than a flat-table lookup','TLBmisscanhitwalkcachehugepage.',pt,o=1)
f(10,'workedExample','Sparse mappings can omit entire lower-level subtrees, with overhead depending on their address distribution. An uncached base-page walk reads one entry per level; paging-structure caches and huge leaves reduce that work. A fully populated tree uses the flat table\'s worth of leaf entries plus upper tables. Page-granularity allocation can still waste space; lazy allocation or suballocation changes actual resident usage.','Universals/empiricalnobodyremoved.',pt)
f(11,'prompt','In a four-level x86-64 model, 50 PostgreSQL backends fully populate an aligned 64 GiB shared region. Assume each backend has independent page-table pages, no HugeTLB PMD sharing, and ignore upper-table overhead. How much leaf-table memory is needed with 4 KiB pages versus 2 MiB pages?','HugeTLBcanPMDshare;modelnottotalobservedmeasurement.',hu)
f(11,'text','About 6.25 GiB with 4 KiB pages; about 12.5 MiB with 2 MiB pages','6400MiB/1024=6.25GiB.',pt,o=1)
f(11,'workedExample','For 4 KiB mappings, each backend needs 64 GiB / 2 MiB = 32,768 leaf PTs. At 4 KiB each that is 128 MiB per backend; 50 × 128 MiB = 6,400 MiB = 6.25 GiB. For 2 MiB mappings, 64 PDs × 4 KiB = 256 KiB per backend; 50 copies total 12.5 MiB. These are leaf-table estimates under the stated no-sharing assumption. Linux HugeTLB may share PMD tables for eligible mappings, and actual memory also depends on population, alignment and upper levels. PostgreSQL documents reduced table overhead as a benefit of huge_pages.','Correctunitandactualsharingassumptions.',hu+['https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/runtime-config-resource.html','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/torvalds/linux/master/mm/hugetlb.c'])
f(11,'explanation','The ordinary data pages are shared, while page-table sharing depends on mapping type and implementation. This question explicitly assumes independent tables; eligible Linux HugeTLB mappings may share PMD tables.','Notallsharedmappingsunsharedtables.',hu,o=2)
f(12,'prompt','The scheduler switches between tasks with different userspace memory contexts on x86-64. Which hardware register selects the new address-space root?','ProcessesCLONE_VM/kthreadsharedcontextexceptions.',pt)
f(12,'workedExample','CR3 selects the physical page-table root and, when enabled, a PCID. Switching memory contexts requires selecting the appropriate root and maintaining TLB coherence; details include PCID/no-flush rules and KPTI. Tasks sharing the same memory context need no root change solely because their task identity changed. PCIDs permit retaining tagged translations only when the kernel also obeys invalidation/reuse rules.','Notjustonemovforallcontextswitch.',pt)
